That's interesting. At the Ohio State University, I'm pretty sure that
'Professor' is an official title of someone employed by the University as a
teacher. They have a PhD and are expected to publish books, articles, do
research, etc.

But, there are grad studuents, who are officially called "Teaching
Assistants" or T.A.s, and have an advisor (who's a professor). They are
working on their master's or PhD. They usually teach lower level classes, or
do the 'recitation' (Some classes have a 'lecture' part, which is given by a
professor, usually in an auditorium of over 200 people (typically low level
classes that eveyone takes, like statistics or biology), and then a
'recitation' part, which is taught by a TA in the department, and is about
20-30 students.)

But once you get into higher level classes, or honors classes, they are all
taught by professors. We really don't use the term 'lecturer'.

And so far as I've seen, everyone calls the professor "Dr. So-and-so", and
usually the TA by the first name.

